<DocsLayout title="How limiting works" description="Learn how RedstoneLimiter counts redstone activity in blocks, subchunks, chunks and regions and how rolling windows and sustain work.">
<div class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow">THE MECHANICS</div><h1 class="page-title">How limiting works</h1><p class="lead">RedstoneLimiter watches circuit activity in four nested areas. Understanding the size of each area is the key to sensible limits. The short animations below show exactly how the settings work.</p></div>
<div class="prose">
<h2 id="scope">The four scopes <a href="#scope" class="anchor">#</a></h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Actual size</th><th>What it answers</th><th>Default base max</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Block</strong></td><td>One exact x/y/z position</td><td>Is this particular device firing too often?</td><td>Per material (e.g. piston 3)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Subchunk</strong></td><td>16 × 16 × 16 blocks</td><td>Is a compact part of the machine too busy?</td><td>96</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chunk</strong></td><td>16 × 16 block footprint, full height</td><td>Is the total redstone activity in this chunk too high?</td><td>1024</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>32 × 32 chunks, full height</td><td>Is a very large area collectively too active?</td><td>96</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Potential surprise:</strong> A single region covers <strong>1,024 chunks</strong> horizontally. Its default base max of 96 is shared across the region, not 96 per chunk. If only a local machine should be controlled, consider your region settings carefully. A region is not a Folia execution region.</div>
<h2 id="sequence">Which limit gets checked first? <a href="#sequence" class="anchor">#</a></h2>
<div class="timeline"><div class="timepoint"><i></i>BLOCK</div><div class="timepoint"><i></i>SUBCHUNK</div><div class="timepoint"><i></i>CHUNK</div><div class="timepoint"><i></i>REGION</div></div>
<p>Each event is counted at the reached scope. The engine checks block → subchunk → chunk → region, and <strong>returns as soon as a scope blocks</strong>. A blocked block activation therefore does not also advance that attempt through all broader counters.</p>
<AnimatedDiagram slug="01-scope-cascade" title="Watch: which scope checks an activation?" description="Follow each activation from its individual block to the wider areas. When one scope blocks an activation, that attempt does not advance into the broader counters." note="Demonstration maxima are deliberately smaller than the plugin defaults so you can see the blocking behavior." />
<h2 id="period">What does a period mean? <a href="#period" class="anchor">#</a></h2>
<Code code={`period:\n  duration-millis: 5000  # Count activity over 5 seconds\n  sustain-millis: 2000   # Temporarily block for at least 2 seconds\n  sliding: true         # Rolling window, not a fixed reset`} />
<p>With a configured max of 5 and no multiplier: activations 1–5 are allowed. <strong>Activation 6 is blocked</strong>. Further over-limit attempts are still counted at that scope and can extend its temporary blocked-until time.</p>
<AnimatedDiagram slug="02-activity-period" title="Watch: rolling window versus fixed period" description="The upper timeline counts recent activations in small time buckets. The lower timeline resets its counter when the fixed period expires." note="The demo uses a 5-second period. It illustrates counting behavior, not real-time server TPS." />
<div class="grid-2"><div class="card"><div class="mini-label">SLIDING: TRUE</div><h3>Rolling window</h3><p>Look at the recent duration (using bounded time buckets). Old activity gradually falls out of the count.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="mini-label">SLIDING: FALSE</div><h3>Fixed window</h3><p>Use a period that resets when its configured duration has elapsed. Existing sustain can continue beyond the reset.</p></div></div>
<p>The duration controls <strong>counting</strong>. Sustain controls <strong>temporary blocking</strong>. Neither setting permanently quarantines chunks.</p>
<AnimatedDiagram slug="03-threshold-sustain" title="Watch: thresholds and temporary sustain blocking" description="See the moment the counter exceeds its maximum and the temporary block begins. More over-limit attempts can extend the deadline; the delay is not permanent quarantine." note="Demo maximum is 3. Your actual threshold comes from the configured scope, preset and TPS multiplier." />
<h2 id="events">Which game events are observed? <a href="#events" class="anchor">#</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Redstone current transitions:</strong> event counts when the current actually changes. A blocked transition restores the old current.</li><li><strong>Observers:</strong> activity observed through block-physics events, with a configurable deduplication period. Those physics events cannot themselves be cancelled by the listener.</li><li><strong>Dispensers and droppers:</strong> monitored through dispense events.</li><li><strong>Pistons:</strong> extend and retract events are monitored. Maximum push length is checked separately by <code>max_piston_push</code>.</li></ul>
<h2 id="multiplier">How do preset and TPS influence the max? <a href="#multiplier" class="anchor">#</a></h2>
<Code title="Effective threshold (source formula)" code={`effectiveMax = ceil(baseMax × presetMultiplier × tpsMultiplier)\n\nbaseMax = 0 → unlimited for that scope / material`} />
<p><code>STRICT</code> = 1×, <code>BALANCED</code> = 1.5×, <code>LENIENT</code> = 2×. With default TPS scaling, ≤16.0 TPS uses 1×, ≥19.5 uses 2×, and TPS in between interpolates. These multipliers alter the maximum count, <strong>not</strong> the time window or sustain.</p>
<AnimatedDiagram slug="04-multipliers" title="Watch: how a base limit becomes the effective limit" description="The threshold can increase with a more permissive preset and healthy TPS. This animation shows the formula and the moving TPS multiplier." note="Shows the uploaded default TPS-scaling values; changing the YAML changes the result." />
<h2 id="exception">What about whitelists and quarantine? <a href="#exception" class="anchor">#</a></h2>
<ul><li><strong>Whitelisted chunks:</strong> record telemetry but do not automatically threshold-block. The independent piston push-size rule can still cancel an extension.</li><li><strong>Manually quarantined chunks:</strong> block monitored cancellable circuit activity across the chunk, overriding whitelist entries. Observer physics events cannot themselves be cancelled. They remain quarantined until an administrator changes the policy.</li><li><strong>Disabled worlds:</strong> skip counting, blocking, visual effects and notifications completely.</li></ul><p>None of these are automatically added to manual quarantine when a temporary activity limit is exceeded.</p>
<AnimatedDiagram slug="05-chunk-policy" title="Watch: normal, whitelisted and quarantined chunks" description="A whitelisted chunk skips automatic blocking while collecting activity telemetry. Manual quarantine blocks monitored, cancellable activity. Normal chunks use the four-scope limiter." note="The diagram simplifies event handling. Observer physics events are monitored but are not themselves cancellable." />
